From 578c95c600711540f496580468279625ff5fec39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 21:05:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] JavaScriptCore: JSON.parse through json.library on the 68k

On PLATFORM(AMIGA) and OS(AMIGAOS3) (the browser and javascript.library's
engine), JSON.parse, JSON.parse with a reviver, JSON.rawJSON and the C++
JSONParse calls parse with json.library's JSONParse into one flat block,
then make the values in one loop over its nodes (JSONLibraryParser.cpp).
"__proto__" is a plain own property, the last of equal keys wins in the
first one's place, numbers are correctly rounded (32-bit integers taken
straight), unpaired surrogates are read again from the source text, a
reviver gets each value's source text, and syntax errors give the line
and column. Nesting deeper than 1024 levels is the RangeError a reviver's
parse gave. LiteralParser is not built there, nor eval's JSON and JSONP
fast paths (ordinary parsing does those programs). json.library is opened
once (LIBS:, then PROGDIR:Libs/); without it JSON.parse throws an Error
that says so.

The fast stringifier also checks the stack on the 68k: GCC makes
append()'s frame bigger than its 384-byte estimate, and with no guard page
a deep or cyclic value ran off the stack.

